<div>
    <div class="mb-16">
        <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white sm:text-5xl mb-6">
            <span class="bg-gradient-to-r from-amber-400 via-orange-400 to-orange-400 bg-clip-text text-transparent">Internationalization</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-8">
            Build multilingual applications with ease. Soli provides built-in i18n support for translations, number formatting, currency, dates, and pluralization.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="my-8 p-6 rounded-xl bg-amber-500/10 border border-amber-500/20 mb-12">
        <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-amber-400 mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">
            <svg class="h-5 w-5" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-width="1.5" stroke="currentColor">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M12 21a9.004 9.004 0 008.716-6.747M12 21a9.004 9.004 0 01-8.716-6.747M12 21c2.485 0 4.5-4.03 4.5-9S14.485 3 12 3m0 18c-2.485 0-4.5-4.03-4.5-9S9.515 3 12 3m0 0a8.997 8.997 0 017.843 4.582M12 3a8.997 8.997 0 00-7.843 4.582m15.686 0A11.953 11.953 0 0112 10.5c-2.998 0-5.74-1.1-7.843-2.918m15.686 0A8.959 8.959 0 0121 12c0 .778-.099 1.533-.284 2.253m0 0A17.919 17.919 0 0112 16.5c-3.162 0-6.133-.815-8.716-2.247m0 0A9.015 9.015 0 013 12c0-1.605.42-3.113 1.157-4.418" />
            </svg>
            Global by Design
        </h4>
        <p class="text-amber-200/80 text-sm">
            The <code>I18n</code> class provides everything you need to localize your application: string translations with interpolation, pluralization rules, locale-aware number and currency formatting, and date localization.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">Setting the Locale</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">Use <code>I18n.locale()</code> to get the current locale and <code>I18n.set_locale()</code> to change it.</p>

    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-12">
        <label class="block px-4 py-2 bg-white/5 text-xs text-gray-400 font-mono border-b border-white/5">Locale Management</label>
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm"># Get the current locale (defaults to "en")
current = I18n.locale()
print(current);  # "en"

# Set locale to French
I18n.set_locale("fr")
print(I18n.locale());  # "fr"

# Set locale to German
I18n.set_locale("de");</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">Basic Translation</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">Translate strings using <code>I18n.translate(key, locale?, translations?)</code>. The function looks up the key in your translations hash.</p>

    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-12">
        <label class="block px-4 py-2 bg-white/5 text-xs text-gray-400 font-mono border-b border-white/5">String Translation</label>
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm"># Define translations
translations = {
  "en.greeting" => "Hello",
  "fr.greeting" => "Bonjour",
  "de.greeting" => "Hallo",
  "es.greeting" => "Hola",
  "en.welcome" => "Welcome to our app!",
  "fr.welcome" => "Bienvenue dans notre application!"
}

# Translate using current locale
I18n.set_locale("fr")
msg = I18n.translate("greeting", null, translations)
print(msg);  # "Bonjour"

# Translate with explicit locale
hello = I18n.translate("greeting", "de", translations)
print(hello);  # "Hallo"

# Falls back to key if not found
missing = I18n.translate("unknown_key", null, translations)
print(missing);  # "unknown_key"</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">Pluralization</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">Handle singular and plural forms with <code>I18n.plural(key, n, locale?, translations?)</code>. Use <code>_zero</code> suffix for zero, <code>_one</code> for singular, and <code>_other</code> for plural.</p>

    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-12">
        <label class="block px-4 py-2 bg-white/5 text-xs text-gray-400 font-mono border-b border-white/5">Pluralization</label>
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm">translations = {
  "en.item_zero" => "No items",
  "en.item_one" => "1 item",
  "en.item_other" => "{count} items",
  "fr.item_one" => "{count} article",
  "fr.item_other" => "{count} articles"
}

I18n.set_locale("en")
# Zero form
none = I18n.plural("item", 0, null, translations)
print(none);  # "No items"

# Singular form
single = I18n.plural("item", 1, null, translations)
print(single);  # "1 item"

# Plural form
multiple = I18n.plural("item", 5, null, translations)
print(multiple);  # "{count} items"

# French plural
fr_plural = I18n.plural("item", 3, "fr", translations)
print(fr_plural);  # "{count} articles"</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">Number Formatting</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">Format numbers according to locale conventions using <code>I18n.format_number(n, locale?)</code>.</p>

    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-12">
        <label class="block px-4 py-2 bg-white/5 text-xs text-gray-400 font-mono border-b border-white/5">Number Formatting</label>
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm">value = 1234.56

# English format (dot as decimal separator)
en_num = I18n.format_number(value, "en")
print(en_num);  # "1234.56"

# French format (comma as decimal separator)
fr_num = I18n.format_number(value, "fr")
print(fr_num);  # "1234,56"

# German format
de_num = I18n.format_number(value, "de")
print(de_num);  # "1234,56"</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">Currency Formatting</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">Format monetary amounts with <code>I18n.format_currency(amount, currency, locale?)</code>. Supports common currency codes.</p>

    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-12">
        <label class="block px-4 py-2 bg-white/5 text-xs text-gray-400 font-mono border-b border-white/5">Currency Formatting</label>
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm">price = 1999.99

# US Dollars
usd = I18n.format_currency(price, "USD", "en")
print(usd);  # "$1,999.99"

# Euros with French locale
eur = I18n.format_currency(price, "EUR", "fr")
print(eur);  # "1.999,99 €"  (plain space before €)

# British Pounds
gbp = I18n.format_currency(price, "GBP", "en")
print(gbp);  # "£1,999.99"

# Japanese Yen
jpy = I18n.format_currency(2500, "JPY", "en")
print(jpy);  # "¥2,500"</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">Date Formatting</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">Format dates using <code>I18n.format_date(timestamp, locale?)</code>. Different locales use different date formats.</p>

    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-12">
        <label class="block px-4 py-2 bg-white/5 text-xs text-gray-400 font-mono border-b border-white/5">Date Formatting</label>
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm"># Unix timestamp for January 15, 2026
timestamp = 1768521600

# US format: MM/DD/YYYY
us_date = I18n.format_date(timestamp, "en")
print(us_date);  # "01/15/2026"

# French format: DD/MM/YYYY
fr_date = I18n.format_date(timestamp, "fr")
print(fr_date);  # "15/01/2026"

# German format: DD.MM.YYYY
de_date = I18n.format_date(timestamp, "de")
print(de_date);  # "15.01.2026"

# ISO format (default for other locales)
iso_date = I18n.format_date(timestamp, "ja")
print(iso_date);  # "2026-01-15"</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">DateTime.format() with Locale</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">The <code>DateTime.format()</code> method accepts an optional locale parameter to produce localized month and day names. This is useful when you need full control over the format pattern while still getting translated names.</p>

    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-12">
        <label class="block px-4 py-2 bg-white/5 text-xs text-gray-400 font-mono border-b border-white/5">DateTime Locale Formatting</label>
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm">dt = DateTime.parse("2024-03-06T14:30:00Z")

# English (default)
dt.format("%A %d %B %Y")         # "Wednesday 06 March 2024"

# French
dt.format("%A %d %B %Y", "fr")   # "mercredi 06 mars 2024"
dt.format("%d %b %Y", "fr")      # "06 mars 2024"

# Spanish
dt.format("%A %d %B %Y", "es")   # "miércoles 06 marzo 2024"

# Supported locales: "en", "fr", "es", "de", "it", "pt"
# Numeric specifiers (%Y, %m, %d, %H, %M, %S) are unaffected by locale</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 id="translation-files" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 scroll-mt-24">Translation Files</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6"><code class="text-cyan-400">config/locales/*.yml</code> files are loaded when the server starts, each keyed by its locale, and <code class="text-cyan-400">I18n.translate(key)</code> reads them when you pass no translations hash.</p>

    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
        <label class="block px-4 py-2 bg-white/5 text-xs text-gray-400 font-mono border-b border-white/5">config/locales/fr.yml</label>
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-yaml text-sm">fr:
  nav:
    home: "Accueil"</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm">I18n.translate("nav.home", "fr")   # "Accueil"</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">Under <code class="text-cyan-400">soli serve --dev</code> an edit to one of these files &mdash; a new key, a new locale file, even a <code class="text-cyan-400">config/locales/</code> directory created after boot &mdash; is picked up on save, with no restart: the workers reload the translations and the browser reloads the page. In production they are read once, at boot. <code class="text-cyan-400">soli test</code> loads them too, so a spec sees the application&rsquo;s messages and names.</p>

    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">A JSON file works too, loaded by hand and passed as the translations hash:</p>

    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-8">
        <label class="block px-4 py-2 bg-white/5 text-xs text-gray-400 font-mono border-b border-white/5">config/locales/en.json</label>
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-json text-sm">{
  "en.app_name": "My Application",
  "en.nav.home": "Home",
  "en.nav.about": "About Us",
  "en.nav.contact": "Contact",
  "en.messages.success": "Operation completed successfully!",
  "en.messages.error": "An error occurred. Please try again.",
  "en.user.greeting": "Hello, {name}!",
  "en.items_one": "You have 1 item",
  "en.items_other": "You have {count} items"
}</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-12">
        <label class="block px-4 py-2 bg-white/5 text-xs text-gray-400 font-mono border-b border-white/5">Loading Translations</label>
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm"># Load translations from JSON file
en_json = file_read("config/locales/en.json")
en_translations = json_decode(en_json)
fr_json = file_read("config/locales/fr.json")
fr_translations = json_decode(fr_json)
# Merge translations
all_translations = en_translations.merge(fr_translations)
# Use in your application
greeting = I18n.translate("nav.home", "en", all_translations)
print(greeting);  # "Home"</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 id="fallbacks" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 scroll-mt-24">Fallbacks between locales</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">A key missing in the request&rsquo;s locale is looked up along a chain: the locale, the fallbacks you declare for it, its base language (<code class="text-cyan-400">fr-CA</code> &rarr; <code class="text-cyan-400">fr</code>), the default locale, then English. <code class="text-cyan-400">I18n.translate</code>, <code class="text-cyan-400">I18n.plural</code>, the validation messages and the model and attribute names all follow it.</p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm">I18n.set_fallbacks({"ca": ["es"], "pt-BR": "pt"})   # per application
I18n.locale_chain("ca")                             # ["ca", "es", "en"]
I18n.fallbacks()                                    # {ca =&gt; [es], pt-BR =&gt; [pt]}</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">Call <code class="text-cyan-400">I18n.set_fallbacks</code> once, at boot (an initializer, or the top of <code class="text-cyan-400">config/routes.sl</code>). It replaces what was declared before.</p>
    <h2 id="validation-messages" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 scroll-mt-24">Validation messages</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">A model&rsquo;s validation errors read in the request&rsquo;s locale. Soli ships the messages for <code class="text-cyan-400">en</code>, <code class="text-cyan-400">fr</code>, <code class="text-cyan-400">de</code>, <code class="text-cyan-400">es</code>, <code class="text-cyan-400">it</code> and <code class="text-cyan-400">pt</code>; an application with no locale file gets them as they are:</p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm">I18n.set_locale("fr")
post = Post.new({"title": ""})
post.save
post._errors        # [{field =&gt; title, message =&gt; doit être rempli(e)}]
post.full_messages  # ["Title doit être rempli(e)"]</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">Each message has a code. Its sentence is looked up, in each locale of the chain, under these keys &mdash; the first one found wins:</p>
    <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-2 text-gray-400 text-sm mb-6">
        <li><code class="text-cyan-400">models.&lt;model&gt;.errors.attributes.&lt;field&gt;.&lt;code&gt;</code> &mdash; one field of one model</li>
        <li><code class="text-cyan-400">models.&lt;model&gt;.errors.&lt;code&gt;</code> &mdash; every field of one model</li>
        <li><code class="text-cyan-400">errors.messages.&lt;code&gt;</code> &mdash; in your <code class="text-cyan-400">config/locales/*.yml</code></li>
        <li><code class="text-cyan-400">errors.messages.&lt;code&gt;</code> &mdash; in the messages Soli ships</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6"><code class="text-cyan-400">&lt;model&gt;</code> is the class name in snake case (<code class="text-cyan-400">BlogPost</code> &rarr; <code class="text-cyan-400">blog_post</code>, <code class="text-cyan-400">Admin::User</code> &rarr; <code class="text-cyan-400">admin/user</code>).</p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead><tr class="border-b border-white/10"><th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-white">Code</th><th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-white">English</th><th class="text-left py-2 px-3 text-white">From</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 px-3"><code class="text-cyan-400">blank</code></td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-gray-400">can&#x27;t be blank</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-gray-400">presence, a NOT NULL column</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 px-3"><code class="text-cyan-400">taken</code></td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-gray-400">has already been taken</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-gray-400">uniqueness, a unique index</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 px-3"><code class="text-cyan-400">invalid</code></td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-gray-400">is invalid</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-gray-400">format, a custom validator returning false</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 px-3"><code class="text-cyan-400">not_a_number</code></td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-gray-400">is not a number</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-gray-400">numericality</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 px-3"><code class="text-cyan-400">too_short</code></td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-gray-400">is too short (minimum is {count} characters)</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-gray-400">min_length</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 px-3"><code class="text-cyan-400">too_long</code></td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-gray-400">is too long (maximum is {count} characters)</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-gray-400">max_length</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 px-3"><code class="text-cyan-400">greater_than_or_equal_to</code></td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-gray-400">must be greater than or equal to {count}</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-gray-400">min</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 px-3"><code class="text-cyan-400">less_than_or_equal_to</code></td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-gray-400">must be less than or equal to {count}</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-gray-400">max</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 px-3"><code class="text-cyan-400">inclusion</code></td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-gray-400">is not included in the list</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-gray-400">inclusion / one_of</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 px-3"><code class="text-cyan-400">wrong_type</code></td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-gray-400">must be {type}</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-gray-400">type: — {type} reads errors.types.&lt;type&gt;</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 px-3"><code class="text-cyan-400">value_object / value_object_list</code></td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-gray-400">must be a {class} / must be a list of {class}</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-gray-400">value_field</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 px-3"><code class="text-cyan-400">foreign_key</code></td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-gray-400">must reference an existing record</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-gray-400">a foreign key</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 px-3"><code class="text-cyan-400">still_referenced</code></td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-gray-400">is still referenced by other records</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-gray-400">a restricted delete</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-2 px-3"><code class="text-cyan-400">stale</code></td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-gray-400">is stale: the record was changed since it was read — reload it and try again</td><td class="py-2 px-3 text-gray-400">optimistic_lock</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12"><code class="text-cyan-400">too_short</code> and <code class="text-cyan-400">too_long</code> are plural keys (<code class="text-cyan-400">too_short_one</code>, <code class="text-cyan-400">too_short_other</code>, &hellip;, chosen by the CLDR rules of the locale); a plain <code class="text-cyan-400">too_short</code> key is used for every count when you write one.</p>
    <h3 id="overriding-messages" class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4 scroll-mt-24">Overriding a message, adding a language</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">Your locale files sit on top of the shipped messages, key by key. Write only what you change:</p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
        <label class="block px-4 py-2 bg-white/5 text-xs text-gray-400 font-mono border-b border-white/5">config/locales/fr.yml</label>
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-yaml text-sm">fr:
  errors:
    messages:
      blank: "est obligatoire"          # every model
  models:
    blog_post:
      errors:
        taken: "existe déjà"            # every field of BlogPost
        attributes:
          slug:
            taken: "est pris par un autre article"</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">A language Soli does not ship works as soon as a file declares it. A code the file leaves out falls back along the chain, then to English, never to nothing:</p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
        <label class="block px-4 py-2 bg-white/5 text-xs text-gray-400 font-mono border-b border-white/5">config/locales/nl.yml</label>
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-yaml text-sm">nl:
  errors:
    messages:
      blank: "moet opgegeven zijn"
      too_short_one: "is te kort (minimaal {count} teken)"
      too_short_other: "is te kort (minimaal {count} tekens)"</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6"><code class="text-cyan-400">soli i18n:export fr</code> writes the shipped French messages to <code class="text-cyan-400">config/locales/soli.fr.yml</code> as a starting point, and <code class="text-cyan-400">soli i18n:missing nl</code> lists the keys <code class="text-cyan-400">nl</code> still lacks against your default locale (<code class="text-cyan-400">--from en</code> to choose), exiting 1 when something is missing:</p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-12">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-bash text-sm">soli i18n:export fr              # config/locales/soli.fr.yml (--force to overwrite)
soli i18n:missing nl             # nl: 3 of 25 keys of en missing …
soli i18n:missing de --from fr</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <h3 id="custom-messages" class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4 scroll-mt-24">Your own messages</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6"><code class="text-cyan-400">message:</code> replaces what a rule says. A String is the sentence in every locale; a Symbol is a key, looked up like a code:</p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm">class Post &lt; Model
  validates("slug", {"format": "^[a-z-]+$", "message": :slug_format})
  validates("code", {"format": "^[A-Z]+$", "message": "must be upper case"})
end</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-yaml text-sm">fr:
  errors:
    messages:
      slug_format: "ne doit contenir que des minuscules et des tirets"</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">A custom validator may return a Symbol too (<code class="text-cyan-400">return :too_big if size &gt; 10</code>), or push <code class="text-cyan-400">{"field": "size", "message": :too_big}</code> onto <code class="text-cyan-400">@_errors</code>. A key nothing translates shows as the key itself, so the gap is visible. <code class="text-cyan-400">I18n.translate("errors.messages.taken", "de")</code> reads the shipped messages as well.</p>
    <h2 id="model-and-attribute-names" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 scroll-mt-24">Model and attribute names</h2>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
        <label class="block px-4 py-2 bg-white/5 text-xs text-gray-400 font-mono border-b border-white/5">config/locales/fr.yml</label>
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-yaml text-sm">fr:
  models:
    blog_post:
      name_one: "Article"
      name_other: "Articles"
      attributes:
        title: "Titre"</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm">BlogPost.human_name                      # "Article" ("Blog post" with no key)
BlogPost.human_name(3)                   # "Articles"
BlogPost.human_attribute_name("title")   # "Titre" ("Title" with no key)
post.full_messages                       # ["Titre doit être rempli(e)", …]</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12"><code class="text-cyan-400">full_messages</code> joins the name and the message with <code class="text-cyan-400">errors.format</code> (<code class="text-cyan-400">"{attribute} {message}"</code> in every shipped locale); an error on <code class="text-cyan-400">_base</code> is its message alone. The form builder uses both: <code class="text-cyan-400">f.label("title")</code> reads the translated name when the form&rsquo;s record is a model, and <code class="text-cyan-400">f.error_summary()</code> lists the full messages &mdash; &ldquo;Title can&rsquo;t be blank&rdquo; rather than &ldquo;can&rsquo;t be blank&rdquo;.</p>
    <h2 id="translated-model-fields" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 scroll-mt-24">Translated model fields</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">A model field can hold one value per locale. Declare it in the class body with <code class="text-cyan-400">translate</code> (or <code class="text-cyan-400">translates</code>, the Globalize/Mobility spelling), and the field reads and writes in the request&rsquo;s locale. <code class="text-cyan-400">Article.translate("title", "body")</code> after the class does the same.</p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
        <label class="block px-4 py-2 bg-white/5 text-xs text-gray-400 font-mono border-b border-white/5">app/models/article.sl</label>
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm">class Article &lt; Model
  translate "title", "body"
end

article = Article.create({"title": "Hello", "body": "Text"})

I18n.set_locale("fr")
article.title                 # "Hello" — no French yet, the column answers
article.title = "Bonjour"
article.title                 # "Bonjour" — read back before save
article.save

I18n.set_locale("de")
Article.find(article._key).title   # "Hello"
I18n.set_locale("fr")
Article.find(article._key).title   # "Bonjour"</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">Where the values live:</p>
    <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-400 mb-6 space-y-2">
        <li><strong class="text-white">The column</strong> (<code class="text-cyan-400">title</code>) keeps what <code class="text-cyan-400">create</code> / <code class="text-cyan-400">new</code> were given. It is the fallback: a locale with no translation reads it.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white"><code class="text-cyan-400">translated_fields</code></strong> holds the rest, as <code class="text-cyan-400">{"title": {"fr": "Bonjour", "es": "Hola"}}</code>, in the same document. A <code class="text-cyan-400">create</code> can seed it directly.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">A write</strong> &mdash; <code class="text-cyan-400">article.title = …</code>, <code class="text-cyan-400">@title = …</code> in a method, <code class="text-cyan-400">article.update({"title": …})</code>, <code class="text-cyan-400">article.save({"title": …})</code> &mdash; is staged under the locale active <em>when you write</em>, and <code class="text-cyan-400">save</code> / <code class="text-cyan-400">update</code> merge it into <code class="text-cyan-400">translated_fields</code>, keeping the other locales already stored. It does not overwrite the column, even in the default locale.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">A read looks along the locale&rsquo;s regional chain &mdash; the locale, its declared fallbacks, its base language (<code class="text-cyan-400">fr-CA</code> &rarr; <code class="text-cyan-400">fr</code>) &mdash; for a staged value, then a stored one, and then reads the column. The default locale is not part of that chain: the column already holds the record&rsquo;s own language.</p>
    <h3 id="several-locales" class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4 scroll-mt-24">Reading and writing several locales</h3>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm">article.translation("title", "fr")         # "Bonjour" — that locale only, nil if none
article.translations("title")              # {fr =&gt; Bonjour, es =&gt; Hola}
article.set_translation("title", "de", "Hallo")   # staged; save writes it</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">A form can edit several locales at once. <code class="text-cyan-400">{"locale": "fr"}</code> on a field names it <code class="text-cyan-400">translated_fields[title][fr]</code> and fills it with that translation; <code class="text-cyan-400">update(params)</code> merges the hash it produces locale by locale:</p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-erb text-sm">&lt;%- f.label("title", "Titre", {"locale": "fr"}) %&gt;
&lt;%- f.text_field("title", {"locale": "fr"}) %&gt;
&lt;%- f.text_field("title", {"locale": "es"}) %&gt;</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-12">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm"># PATCH /articles/:id
def update(req)
  @article = Article.find(params["id"])
  locales = {"fr": true, "es": true}   # permit names each locale it lets through
  @article.update(permit(params, {"translated_fields": {"title": locales}}))
end</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <h3 id="querying-translations" class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4 scroll-mt-24">Querying translations</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6"><code class="text-cyan-400">where</code> compares the column. <code class="text-cyan-400">where_translated</code> compares what a reader in the current locale sees &mdash; the translation along the regional chain, else the column &mdash; and <code class="text-cyan-400">order_translated</code> sorts by it. Both chain like <code class="text-cyan-400">where</code> and <code class="text-cyan-400">order</code>:</p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm">I18n.set_locale("fr")
Article.where_translated({"title": "Bonjour"}).all
Article.where({"published": true}).order_translated("title", "desc").limit(10).all
Article.missing_translations("es").all   # records lacking es for a translated field</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">These three are SoliDB-only: on the SQL adapters they are refused, as a raw string <code class="text-cyan-400">where</code> is. A path in a string filter reaches one locale directly: <code class="text-cyan-400">Article.where("doc.translated_fields.title.fr == @t", {"t": "Bonjour"})</code>.</p>
    <h2 id="formats-from-locale-files" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 scroll-mt-24">Formats from your locale files</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">A locale file can set the number and date conventions of a language Soli does not format, or change those of one it does. Only the locale&rsquo;s own file (and its regional chain) is read &mdash; a format is never borrowed from the default locale:</p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
        <label class="block px-4 py-2 bg-white/5 text-xs text-gray-400 font-mono border-b border-white/5">config/locales/nl.yml</label>
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-yaml text-sm">nl:
  number:
    separator: ","          # decimal separator
    delimiter: "."          # thousands
    currency:
      format: "{unit} {amount}"
  date:
    format: "%d-%m-%Y"      # strftime</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm">I18n.format_number(1234567.5, "nl")          # "1.234.567,5"
I18n.format_currency(1999.99, "EUR", "nl")   # "€ 1.999,99"
I18n.format_date(timestamp, "nl")            # "16-01-2026"</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12"><code class="text-cyan-400">number.currency.separator</code> / <code class="text-cyan-400">.delimiter</code> override <code class="text-cyan-400">number.*</code> for amounts.</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">Supported Locales</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">The I18n class provides built-in formatting support for these locales:</p>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-12">
        <table class="w-full">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th class="text-left">Locale Code</th>
                    <th class="text-left">Language</th>
                    <th class="text-left">Number Format</th>
                    <th class="text-left">Date Format</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>en</code></td>
                    <td>English</td>
                    <td>1,234.56</td>
                    <td>MM/DD/YYYY</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>fr</code></td>
                    <td>French</td>
                    <td>1.234,56</td>
                    <td>DD/MM/YYYY</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>de</code></td>
                    <td>German</td>
                    <td>1.234,56</td>
                    <td>DD.MM.YYYY</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>es</code></td>
                    <td>Spanish</td>
                    <td>1.234,56</td>
                    <td>YYYY-MM-DD</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>it</code></td>
                    <td>Italian</td>
                    <td>1.234,56</td>
                    <td>YYYY-MM-DD</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">Method Reference</h2>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 mb-12">
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
            <code class="text-orange-300 font-bold">I18n.locale()</code>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mt-2">Returns the current locale string. Defaults to <code>"en"</code> if not set.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
            <code class="text-orange-300 font-bold">I18n.set_locale(locale)</code>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mt-2">Sets the current locale. Returns the locale string.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
            <code class="text-orange-300 font-bold">I18n.translate(key, locale?, translations?)</code>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mt-2">Translates a key. Uses current locale if not specified. Falls back to the key itself if no translation found.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
            <code class="text-orange-300 font-bold">I18n.plural(key, n, locale?, translations?)</code>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mt-2">Returns zero (<code>_zero</code>), singular (<code>_one</code>), or plural (<code>_other</code>)
form based on count.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
            <code class="text-orange-300 font-bold">I18n.format_number(n, locale?)</code>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mt-2">Formats a number with the locale's decimal separator. No thousands grouping, unless the locale file sets <code>number.delimiter</code>.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
            <code class="text-orange-300 font-bold">I18n.format_currency(amount, currency, locale?)</code>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mt-2">Formats a monetary amount with currency symbol. Supports USD, EUR, GBP, JPY.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
            <code class="text-orange-300 font-bold">I18n.format_date(timestamp, locale?)</code>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mt-2">Formats a Unix timestamp as a date string according to locale conventions (<code>date.format</code> from the locale file when set).</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
            <code class="text-orange-300 font-bold">I18n.set_fallbacks(hash) / I18n.fallbacks()</code>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mt-2">The locales tried right after each one, per application.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
            <code class="text-orange-300 font-bold">I18n.locale_chain(locale?)</code>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mt-2">The locales a lookup tries, in order.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
            <code class="text-orange-300 font-bold">Model.human_name(count?) / Model.human_attribute_name(field)</code>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mt-2">The model&rsquo;s / field&rsquo;s name in the current locale, else read as words.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
            <code class="text-orange-300 font-bold">record.full_messages</code>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mt-2">Each validation error as a sentence naming its field.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
            <code class="text-orange-300 font-bold">record.translation(field, locale) / translations(field) / set_translation(field, locale, value)</code>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mt-2">One locale of a translated field / every locale / stage one.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10">
            <code class="text-orange-300 font-bold">Model.where_translated(hash) / order_translated(field, dir?) / missing_translations(locale)</code>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mt-2">Queries on translated fields, as a reader in the locale sees them (SoliDB).</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 id="which-locale" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 scroll-mt-24">Which locale a request runs in</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">A request begins with a locale resolved for it, in this order:</p>
    <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-2 text-gray-400 text-sm mb-4">
        <li>a <code class="text-cyan-400">locale</code> value in the session &mdash; the person picked it;</li>
        <li>a <code class="text-cyan-400">locale</code> cookie;</li>
        <li>the best match for the <code class="text-cyan-400">Accept-Language</code> header <em>among the locales you ship</em> &mdash; <code class="text-cyan-400">fr-CA</code> is served by a <code class="text-cyan-400">fr</code> you have, and a language you have no file for is skipped rather than negotiated to;</li>
        <li><code class="text-cyan-400">SOLI_DEFAULT_LOCALE</code>, or <code class="text-cyan-400">I18n.set_default_locale(...)</code>, defaulting to <code class="text-cyan-400">en</code>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">The locale is restored when the request ends, so it never crosses from one visitor to the next on the same worker &mdash; it used to, and a controller that never called <code class="text-cyan-400">set_locale</code> rendered in whatever language the previous visitor had asked for. Realtime frames (Live View, EUI) resolve it from the socket's session the same way. <code class="text-cyan-400">set_locale</code> still overrides it for the rest of the request.</p>

    <h2 id="cldr-plurals" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 scroll-mt-24">Plurals follow CLDR</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">Which suffix a count selects depends on the language, not only on the number. Soli uses the CLDR categories &mdash; <code class="text-cyan-400">_zero</code>, <code class="text-cyan-400">_one</code>, <code class="text-cyan-400">_two</code>, <code class="text-cyan-400">_few</code>, <code class="text-cyan-400">_many</code>, <code class="text-cyan-400">_other</code> &mdash; and each language uses the ones it has:</p>
    <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-gray-400 text-sm mb-4">
        <li><strong class="text-white">French</strong> has no zero category: <code class="text-cyan-400">0</code> and <code class="text-cyan-400">1</code> are both <code class="text-cyan-400">_one</code> &mdash; &laquo;&nbsp;0 article&nbsp;&raquo;, singular.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">English</strong> has <code class="text-cyan-400">_one</code> and <code class="text-cyan-400">_other</code>; 0 takes <code class="text-cyan-400">_other</code> (&ldquo;0 items&rdquo;).</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">Russian</strong> and <strong class="text-white">Polish</strong> use <code class="text-cyan-400">_few</code> and <code class="text-cyan-400">_many</code>; <strong class="text-white">Arabic</strong> uses all six; <strong class="text-white">Japanese</strong> and <strong class="text-white">Chinese</strong> use only <code class="text-cyan-400">_other</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">Two conveniences on top: a <code class="text-cyan-400">_zero</code> key is still honoured for a count of 0 in <em>any</em> language when you write one, because &ldquo;No items&rdquo; is often worth saying differently; and a category your file does not declare falls back to <code class="text-cyan-400">_other</code> in the same locale before any other language is consulted.</p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">Before this, every language got English&rsquo;s zero/one/other. A French page with a correct <code class="text-cyan-400">fr.yml</code> asked for <code class="text-cyan-400">items_zero</code>, did not find it, and rendered the <em>English</em> string in its place.</p>

    <div class="my-8 p-6 rounded-xl bg-yellow-500/10 border border-yellow-500/20 mb-12">
        <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-yellow-400 mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">
            <svg class="h-5 w-5" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-width="1.5" stroke="currentColor">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M12 9v3.75m9-.75a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0zm-9 3.75h.008v.008H12v-.008z" />
            </svg>
            Best Practice
        </h4>
        <p class="text-yellow-200/80 text-sm">
            You no longer have to. Every request begins with a locale of its own &mdash; a
            <code>locale</code> value in the session, else a <code>locale</code> cookie, else the best
            match for <code>Accept-Language</code> among the locales you ship, else
            <code>SOLI_DEFAULT_LOCALE</code>. <code>set_locale</code> is for overriding that inside a
            request; it no longer has to be called to avoid inheriting the previous visitor's language.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>
